harper: heads-up: found a deterministic goroutine leak in Go generator iterator when canceling during token handshake + auth retry. sharing tiny repro and API suggestion.

harper: repro pattern: call Generator.Stream(ctx) then cancel ctx in a defer or shutdown goroutine. sometimes Next() never returns and process doesn't exit. seen on mac dev + linux CI.

harper: minimal snippet:
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
iter := client.Generator().Stream(ctx, prompt)
go func() {
  for iter.Next() {
    if iter.Value() == "\\n" { cancel() }
  }
  log.Println("iterator end")
}()
// main sometimes blocks on exit waiting for goroutines
```

toni: Err() nil when it hangs? any pprof?

harper: Err() often nil. pprof shows goroutine stuck in http2 read loop around response body.Read(). server log shows auth refresh / retry around same time.

ryan: likely read pump not unblocked when auth-refresh sleeps and caller cancels. UX options: 1) Add Close()/Abort() on iterator to force-local teardown. 2) Make Next() observe ctx.Done() immediately. 3) Add TryNext() non-blocking variant.

toni: prefer explicit Close() on iterator. many sample apps use context.Background() by accident, so relying on ctx cancel is brittle. Close() gives deterministic lifecycle.

harper: prototype workaround: wrapper that returns a Closeable iterator by creating a child ctx for the stream and cancelling that ctx on Close(). works as stopgap.

harper: wrapper sketch:
```go
type CloseIter struct{ It *generator.Iterator; cancel context.CancelFunc }
func (c *CloseIter) Next() bool { return c.It.Next() }
func (c *CloseIter) Value() string { return c.It.Value() }
func (c *CloseIter) Err() error { return c.It.Err() }
func (c *CloseIter) Close() { c.cancel() }
```

ryan: devex action: open issue with repro, pprof, wrapper. engineering action: prototype Add Close() to StreamIterator interface, implement per-stream cancel that closes read pump without affecting shared http.Client. tests: handshake-cancel, auth-retry-cancel, concurrent Close()+Next()+Err() race.

